    "name":  "Colorectal cancer",
    "module":  "oncology",
    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Colorectal cancer (CRC) is the third most common cancer worldwide and the second leading cause of cancer death. It arises from the epithelial lining of the colon or rectum, most often as a progression from adenomatous polyps over 10–15 years. Presentation depends on tumour location: right-sided tumours cause anaemia and weight loss with minimal change in bowel habit; left-sided and rectal tumours cause altered bowel habit, rectal bleeding, and tenesmus. Many cases are detected incidentally via screening. Lynch syndrome and familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) confer high genetic risk.",
